    <DataNode elementId="CPD-3141" textLabel="genistein" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="TZBJGXHYKVUXJN-UHFFFAOYSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="640" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco">Genistein is one of several known isoflavones. Isoflavones, such as genistein and daidzein, are found in a number of plants including lupin, fava beans, soybeans, kudzu, and psoralea being the primary food source. It is also found in the medicinal plant &apos;&apos;Flemingia vestita&apos;&apos; and coffee. Besides functioning as antioxidant and anthelmintic, many isoflavones have been shown to interact with animal and human estrogen receptors, causing effects in the body similar to those caused by the hormone estrogen. Isoflavones also produce non-hormonal effects. Genistein was first isolated in 1899 from the dyer&apos;s broom, &apos;&apos;Genista tinctoria&apos;&apos;; hence, the chemical name derived from the generic name. The compound nucleus was established in 1926, when it was found to be identical with prunetol. It was chemically synthesized in 1928.</Comment>
